## Summary

The State event queue has a successful reentry path, but no coherent failure/recovery transition. If a watcher records a nested state change and then throws, the recorded event stays pending. A later unrelated mutation emits first and only then drains the older event.

On public main `8f2eba12c2c980d0f4d92713106e798891738106`, the observed sequence is **[1, 3, 2] while the current state is 3**. Without the later mutation, event 2 remains undelivered.

This is a bounded design/reliability issue: decide the exception propagation/fan-out policy before implementing it. Do not silently turn watcher errors into successful execution.

## Authority and existing limits

- [C-STATE-0011-E](https://github.com/Proto-UI/Proto-UI/blob/8f2eba12c2c980d0f4d92713106e798891738106/spec/contracts/C-STATE-0011.yaml), **draft**, requires deterministic delivery of reentrant StateEvents without losing recorded transitions.
- [M-STATE-0001](https://github.com/Proto-UI/Proto-UI/blob/8f2eba12c2c980d0f4d92713106e798891738106/spec/modules/M-STATE-0001.yaml), **draft**, owns instance-lifetime slots and deterministic mutation records, independent of host rendering.
- [T-STATE-0004-CASE-REENTRANT-ORDER](https://github.com/Proto-UI/Proto-UI/blob/8f2eba12c2c980d0f4d92713106e798891738106/spec/tests/T-STATE-0004.yaml) maps to [kernel.v0.test.ts](https://github.com/Proto-UI/Proto-UI/blob/8f2eba12c2c980d0f4d92713106e798891738106/packages/modules/state/test/kernel.v0.test.ts), which covers successful FIFO delivery but not a thrown callback with an already-recorded nested mutation.
- Legacy [state-watch.v0.md](https://github.com/Proto-UI/Proto-UI/blob/8f2eba12c2c980d0f4d92713106e798891738106/internal/contracts/state/state-watch.v0.md) deliberately leaves watcher ordering/batching broad. It cannot weaken the cataloged reentrant-record direction, but neither source settles exception aggregation or whether remaining watchers run after one fails. That is the bounded maintainer decision here.

## Minimal reproduction

Put this in a Vitest test alongside `packages/modules/state/test/kernel.v0.test.ts`:

```ts
const kernel = new StateKernel();
const value = kernel.define('value', { kind: 'number.discrete' }, 0);
const seen: number[] = [];
kernel.subscribe(value, event => {
  if (event.type !== 'next') return;
  seen.push(event.next);
  if (event.next === 1) {
    value.set(2);
    throw new Error('consumer failure');
  }
});

expect(() => value.set(1)).toThrow('consumer failure');
expect(value.get()).toBe(2);
expect(seen).toEqual([1]); // transition 1 -> 2 already committed, event stranded
value.set(3);
expect(value.get()).toBe(3);
expect(seen).toEqual([1, 3, 2]); // actual, undesirable recovery order
```

The same reentrant sequence without the throw observes [1, 2, 3]. Executed under Node 22.22.1, pnpm 10.32.1, Vitest 2.1.9 on Windows; no timers. A second probe through an actual RuntimeSession's State port also observes [1, 3, 2], with callbacks correctly entering callback phase and restoring unknown/idle after the exception. [Module watcher forwarding](https://github.com/Proto-UI/Proto-UI/blob/8f2eba12c2c980d0f4d92713106e798891738106/packages/modules/state/src/impl.ts#L87) does not contain or translate the watcher exception. This is distinct from #442's already-fixed callback-scope restoration.

## Cause and design decision

[StateKernel.setById](https://github.com/Proto-UI/Proto-UI/blob/8f2eba12c2c980d0f4d92713106e798891738106/packages/modules/state/src/kernel.ts#L109) commits `rec.value` before event dispatch. Reentrant emission is appended to `pending`; on throw, `finally` only clears `emitting`. The next top-level mutation directly emits its own event before flushing the retained queue.

Define the failed-dispatch state explicitly. Options to assess include completing already-committed FIFO delivery before rethrowing/aggregating consumer failures, or an explicit failed/terminal dispatch state that rejects further mutation until a governed recovery. Dropping committed transition records or replaying them after newer events is not a valid accidental fallback. Do not add setTimeout/microtask retry and call that recovery.

## Acceptance and boundaries

- [ ] Record exception propagation, remaining-watcher fan-out, already-recorded nested events, and recovery semantics.
- [ ] Add deterministic tests for same-state and cross-state reentry, throw before/after nested mutation, later mutation after catch, and disposal during dispatch.
- [ ] Preserve actual transition order under the chosen policy; no silently stranded records or older events delivered after a newer accepted event.
- [ ] Verify Module/Runtime callback-scope restoration and borrowed/observed watcher paths, not only direct kernel subscribers.
- [ ] Preserve no-implicit-render behavior, same-value suppression, setup-only registration, and terminal invalidation; no value-validation/accessor migration scope creep.

Implementation should wait for the small exception-policy checkpoint; this issue does not authorize changing public error behavior or promoting draft semantics. Distinct from #637's deprecated-accessor retirement and #623's Scroll lease reentry. No product change, claim, review approval, or issue closure made. AI-assisted source audit and deterministic probes requested by the current user.


